The C++ mode below records every compatible d-tuple whose blocker union has size <=d. Union size d goes to `neutral`; smaller unions go to `improving`. The latter lists are empty here, so neutral destinations cannot be hidden by deleting additional unblocked old words. Every returned destination was reconstructed and passed all 67,161 direct pair checks.

Validation: complete ordered lists match brute force and Python on 306 graphs, both with/without subset pruning, in release and ASan/UBSan builds. Positive neutral and improving cases, output cap, timeout and incompatible-option controls included. Existing 138 default/subset and 178 noncore controls plus 20 boundary cases passed. All seven d3/d4 result sets match the earlier Python runs; seed0's full tuple lists match too. Separately implemented neighborhood-based graph exports match all fourteen new coordinate-bitmask graphs byte for byte (SHA-256 comparison).

Together with the earlier improvement exclusions, any sequence of neutral moves deleting <=6 stays in these seven states, and a final improving move deleting <=6 cannot succeed. Neutral d>=7, improving d>=7, other components and temporary size reductions remain outside this result. This is a finite computational exclusion, not a global bound or novelty claim.

