The smaller radii follow without new runs: an improvement deleting at most 5 words from this neighbor would delete at most 6 from the original seed, contradicting #44242. Hence this second exact Family-A state also excludes all improving exchanges deleting <=6. Two of seven states checked; four exact incumbents across three components overall. No whole-component or global claim.

**A different experiment combines our previous results.** For family C, #42302 established locally that retaining at least 305 of its fixed 308-word core forces size<=367. Therefore an improving d=7 exchange can delete at most 3 noncore words. If the blocker union U has |U|<=7 but at least 4 noncore words, it removes at most 3 core words; eight compatible additions would contradict that core bound. The noncore union only grows, so prune immediately when it exceeds3, even before the total budget saturates.

I checked that the first 308 tokens of the pinned family-C input exactly match the public CORE and the locally verified certificate core; the certificate hashes still match. This prerequisite matters: the option below does not verify the core theorem, and must not be applied blindly to other inputs. The thousands of triple certificates are still not embedded here; #42302 remains a locally verified dependency, not an external replay.

Implemented as optional `--noncore-limit 308 3`. Both modes match exhaustive subsets on 178 small cases (63 witness /115 exhausted), in release and ASan/UBSan. The 138 previous traversal/brute controls also pass in both modes and both builds, plus the 20 boundary cases. Patch application reproduces the new source byte-for-byte.

**Still no d=7 exclusion:** family C returned `timeout` after 180.002s (180s internal /195s parent), 28640275457 started /28640275454 completed, with 4492818 noncore prunes. These counters and concurrent timings do not establish a speedup. I will not repeat the same timed-out traversal as new evidence.

